      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "Consider the following relation on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)\\}\n$$\n\nWhat are the domain and range of this relation?\n\nThe domain is \n$\\{x\\in $\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,:\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\}$\n\nThe range is {{field-3}}",
      "worked_solution": "Given a relation $R⊆A\\times B,$ \n\n- the *domain* of $R$ is the set containing all elements from $A$ that appear as the first component of the ordered pairs in $R,$ and\n\n- the *range* of $R$ is the set containing all elements from $B$ that appear as the second component of the ordered pairs in $R.$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine the given relation.\n\n- Notice that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ is defined only for $x>1.$ So, the set of first components contains all real numbers greater than $1$:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x>1\\}\n$$\n\nHence, the domain is $\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x>1\\}.$\n\n- Notice that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ is continuous on $R,$ and has neither lower nor upper bounds. So, the set of second components contains all real numbers $R.$\nHence, the range is $R.$",
